    <fr:title text="Modular Arithmetic via Curry-Howard">Modular Arithmetic via Curry-Howard</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Type Theory</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    This note follows Chapter 7 of <fr:link href="/hott-2022/" title="Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory" uri="https://caimeo.space/hott-2022/" display-uri="hott-2022" type="local">Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory</fr:link>.
    It develops elementary arithmetic inside type theory, where a proposition is represented by
    the type of its witnesses and a proof is a term of that type.
</html:p>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
          <fr:month>7</fr:month>
          <fr:day>18</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:title text="Logical operations as type formers">Logical operations as type formers</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
        Conjunction is interpreted by product or dependent-pair types, disjunction by coproducts,
        implication by function types, and quantifiers by dependent types:
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            P\land  Q\rightsquigarrow  P\times  Q,\quad 
            P\lor  Q\rightsquigarrow  P+Q,\quad 
            (P\Rightarrow  Q)\rightsquigarrow (P\to  Q),
        ]]></fr:tex>
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            (\forall _{x:A}P(x))\rightsquigarrow \Pi _{x:A}P(x),\qquad 
            (\exists _{x:A}P(x))\rightsquigarrow \Sigma _{x:A}P(x).
        ]]></fr:tex>
        This is evidence-relevant: an existential proof retains both its witness and the evidence.
    </html:p>
        <html:p>
        For example, divisibility is the type
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            d\mid  n\;:\equiv \;\Sigma _{k:\mathbb {N}}(d\cdot  k=n).
        ]]></fr:tex>
        To prove <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mid  n]]></fr:tex> we construct <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(k,p)]]></fr:tex>; to use such a proof, we eliminate the
        dependent pair and obtain the multiplier and its equation.
    </html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
          <fr:month>7</fr:month>
          <fr:day>18</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:title text="Congruence and finite residues">Congruence and finite residues</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
        Congruence modulo <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex> is expressed without quotienting:
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            x\equiv _k y\;:\equiv \;\Sigma _{a,b:\mathbb {N}}(x+k\cdot  a=y+k\cdot  b).
        ]]></fr:tex>
        Reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity follow by arithmetic on the witnesses, so this is
        a typal equivalence relation.
    </html:p>
        <html:p>
        The standard finite types are defined recursively by
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            \mathsf {Fin}(0):\equiv \mathbf {0},\qquad 
            \mathsf {Fin}(k+1):\equiv \mathbf {1}+\mathsf {Fin}(k).
        ]]></fr:tex>
        Their constructors give canonical representatives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0,\ldots ,k]]></fr:tex>; an inclusion
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\iota _k:\mathsf {Fin}(k)\to \mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex> records the represented natural number.
    </html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
          <fr:month>7</fr:month>
          <fr:day>18</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:title text="Reduction modulo k+1">Reduction modulo <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k+1]]></fr:tex></fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
        Recursion defines a residue map
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            [-]_{k+1}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathsf {Fin}(k+1)
        ]]></fr:tex>
        that cycles after <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k+1]]></fr:tex> steps. It is split surjective: every residue has its canonical
        representative, and reducing that representative returns the original residue.
    </html:p>
        <html:p>
        Transporting addition through this reduction defines
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x+_{k+1}y:\equiv [\iota (x)+\iota (y)]_{k+1}]]></fr:tex>. The residue laws imply the unit,
        associativity, inverse, and commutativity laws. Hence
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Z}/(k+1):\equiv \mathsf {Fin}(k+1)]]></fr:tex> is a cyclic abelian group, generated by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1]]></fr:tex>.
        The construction supplies concrete data, not merely an assertion that a quotient exists.
    </html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
